window.docContents[111].resolve({"/Judgments-and-domains/Dynamic-judgments/#Core-C______--Judgments-and-domains--Dynamic-judgments":{"contents":"The judgment  states that the expression , under the environment  and the store , evaluates to  and yields . The store enters expressions because a function call inside an expression may change it.\n\nThe judgment  states that the expression  denotes the location . It holds for a variable, a dereferenced pointer, a field of an object, a field through a pointer and an element of a vector.\n\nThe judgment  states that the command  yields the control , the environment  and the store . The output environment exists so that a declaration extends  for the following commands, and the block discards the extension when it ends.\n\nThe evaluator runs in the monad , which carries the derivation tree. Each rule application records the instance of the rule it concludes, at its depth in the tree, with the antecedent and the consequent in parts. The function renderTrace then lays the tree out as a derivation is written on the board, the premises over a line of inference, the conclusion under it and the rule name at the right. A legend names the environments , the stores  and the subjects too long for a judgment, so every judgment fits one line, and a subtree wider than the page is written apart under a name .\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Judgments and domains","header":"2.3. Dynamic judgments","id":"/Judgments-and-domains/Dynamic-judgments/#Core-C______--Judgments-and-domains--Dynamic-judgments"}});